--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party programs oxAudit can invoke, and does not distribute
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Binary scanning runs whichever of these external programs you have installed.
oxAudit does not bundle, embed, link against, or redistribute any of them; it
executes a copy you installed yourself, at arm's length. No part of their
licensing attaches to oxAudit as a result.

  cve-bin-tool    GPL-3.0-or-later   https://github.com/ossf/cve-bin-tool
  grype           Apache-2.0         https://github.com/anchore/grype

docker/cve-bin-tool/Dockerfile is a build recipe, not a distribution. The image
it describes is built on your machine and fetches cve-bin-tool from PyPI there.
Do not publish a built image to a registry without meeting the GPL obligations
that would then attach to you.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Detection signatures
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The component signatures in src-tauri/src/binscan/native/signatures.toml are
original work. They were derived from binaries whose versions were known
independently, using tools/derive-signatures.py, and are deliberately not
transcribed from cve-bin-tool's checkers, which are GPL-3.0-or-later. See
docs/binary-signatures.md for the method and its provenance.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
YARA rules and Nuclei templates
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

oxAudit bundles no YARA rules and no Nuclei templates. You import your own, and
each imported pack keeps the licence it was published under: oxAudit records
the licence you enter with the pack and with every run that used it, and does
not alter, relicense or redistribute the content.

A user may import a pack published under a copyleft licence, including the
GPL. As with cve-bin-tool above, that is at arm's length: the pack is copied
into the user's own data directory, is read as data, and is never compiled into
or shipped with oxAudit. Nothing from such a pack is in this repository; the
test fixtures are original work.

The engines that run them are libraries compiled into oxAudit. Their licences,
read from each crate's manifest in the version in Cargo.lock:

  yara-x            BSD-3-Clause            VirusTotal
                    https://github.com/VirusTotal/yara-x
                    The YARA-X rule engine (src-tauri/crates/oxaudit-yara)
  rsa               MIT OR Apache-2.0       RustCrypto Developers
                    Enabled only to satisfy a feature yara-x needs
  hickory-resolver  MIT OR Apache-2.0       The contributors to Hickory DNS
                    https://github.com/hickory-dns/hickory-dns
                    DNS questions for live template scans
  rustls            Apache-2.0 OR ISC OR MIT
                    https://github.com/rustls/rustls
                    TLS handshakes for live template scans
  tokio-rustls      MIT OR Apache-2.0
                    https://github.com/rustls/tokio-rustls
  x509-parser       MIT OR Apache-2.0       Pierre Chifflier
                    https://github.com/rusticata/x509-parser
                    Certificate fields for ssl templates
  serde-saphyr      MIT OR Apache-2.0
                    https://github.com/bourumir-wyngs/serde-saphyr
                    Reads Nuclei template YAML
  md-5              MIT OR Apache-2.0       RustCrypto Developers
                    The md5 template helper

oxAudit is not affiliated with VirusTotal or ProjectDiscovery. YARA and Nuclei
are the names of their respective projects. The Nuclei template format is
implemented independently from its public documentation; no Nuclei source code
is included.

